  "teamId": 135,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 San Diego Padres burst out of the gate with an unmistakable sense of purpose. In the first week of April alone, Ramón Laureano delivered a clutch line-drive single, Manny Machado launched a three-run bomb, and Luis Campusano followed with a timely double — the kind of early-season moments that set a tone. The crescendo came on April 15th, when Jackson Merrill laced a sharp double to left that sent the crowd into a frenzy and posted the highest single-play win probability swing of the entire regular season. San Diego looked like a team destined for something special.\n\nThe optimism was briefly tested in late April and again in late June and early July, when opposing hitters made the Padres pay. A grand slam off Randy Vásquez and a brutal four-run shot off Adrian Morejon were reminders that the road would not be smooth. The bullpen showed vulnerability, and a few costly late-inning collapses in July raised real questions about whether San Diego had the depth to compete deep into the season. The team's 91-71 finish suggests they answered those questions, but it was never easy.\n\nThe second half told a story of resilience. Gavin Sheets had already emerged as a power threat by mid-May, but August became the month where the Padres truly steadied themselves. Jackson Merrill went deep for his 19th home run, Luis Campusano came up clutch twice in a span of weeks, and Jake Cronenworth provided a key insurance single to keep a crucial win alive. Even as Mason Miller surrendered multiple damaging homers in late August, the offense responded, and San Diego held on through a turbulent stretch run to claim a Wild Card berth.\n\nIn the Wild Card round, the Padres were electric. Fernando Tatis Jr. and Manny Machado hit home runs on the same October afternoon, and Machado added a go-ahead double for good measure. Michael King navigated late-inning danger with poise, stranding baserunners and collecting critical outs when the pressure was highest. An Xander Bogaerts solo shot put the finishing touches on the victory. It was a fitting end to a season defined by clutch hitting, hard-won wins, and the unmistakable belief that this group of Padres belonged on the postseason stage.",
